Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 10:56:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02552/11362] [outer-loop] M2 plan->execute->solve: dynamics
 necessary-not-sufficient; naive guidance can HURT

The M2 falsifiable-gate measurement for tonight's improved ObjectDeltaModel: does better
dynamics accuracy (cn04 0.000->0.477) translate into actually SOLVING a level? A/B/C per
game (guided-improved vs guided-degraded-pre-tonight vs blind-random), real-env-confirmed
wins, NO hardcoded goal -- same honest framing as M2-v5 (arc3_m2_solve.py).

RESULT (results/arc3_m2_solve_objectdelta.json, budget 400, seed 0):
  cn04: improved 0 | degraded 0 | blind 0
  sp80: improved 0 | degraded 0 | BLIND 1   <-- blind random solved; model-guided did NOT
  ar25: improved 0 | degraded 0 | blind 0
  ka59: improved 0 | degraded 0 | blind 0

TWO honest findings:
1. NO model-guided solve. The improved dynamics model is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT --
   GOAL INDUCTION is the binding wall. Confirms + EXTENDS the M2-v5 vc33 diagnosis to the
   movement games: a good forward model does not, by itself, cross the solve gate.
2. Naive model-guidance can be ANTI-helpful: on sp80 (win = 'move-right x3 then COMMIT'),
   the novelty/change-seeking guided policy DEPRIORITIZED the commit action (which barely
   changes the visible grid) -- the very action that wins -- while blind random stumbled
   into it. So 'prefer state-change/novelty' is not goal-directed planning and here it was
   WORSE than random. The efficiency thesis (model-as-pruner) needs a GOAL-aware progress
   signal (like M2-v5's vc33 progress bar), not generic novelty.

IMPLICATION: tonight's dynamics gains are real and necessary, but the next investment is
GOAL INDUCTION (a per-game target/progress signal from the rare observed wins, e.g. via the
existing arc_agi3_goal_induction.induce_goal_predicate) + goal-directed planning -- NOT more
dynamics accuracy and NOT naive novelty guidance. Adversarial-verify: clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
